\subsection{Why GNN for question answering?}
\label{sec:method-why-gnn}
We provide more discussion on why we use a GNN for solving question answering and reasoning tasks.

Recent work shows that GNNs are effective for modeling various graph algorithms \cite{xu2019can}. Examples of graph algorithms include knowledge graph reasoning, such as execution of logical queries on a KG \cite{Gentner1983, ren2020beta}:
\begin{align*}
    V_?. ~ \exists V\!: ~&\text{Located}(\text{Europe}, V) \\
    \wedge & \neg \text{Held}(\text{World Cup}, V) \wedge \text{President}(V, V_?)
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
\textit{(``Who are the presents of European countries}\\[-0.8mm]
\textit{that have \textbf{not} held the World Cup?'')}
\end{align*}
Viewing such logical queries as input ``questions'', we conducted
a pilot study where we apply \methodname to learn the task of executing logical queries on a KG---including complex queries that contain negation or multi-hop relations about entities.
In this task, we find that \methodname significantly outperforms a baseline model that only uses an LM but not a GNN:

